<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ecumenical Patriarchate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, styled Ecumenical Patriarch, is the archbishop of Constantinople and senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a>. The patriarch oversaw churches across the Byzantine world and, under Ottoman rule, led the Orthodox community of the empire. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> several Balkan churches moved between his authority and patriarchates of their own, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> and Peć.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Υπουργείο Εξωτερικών · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%85_%D0%92%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%84%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%B9_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Orthodox Church, made autocephalous by the Greek state in 1833 and recognised by the <b>Patriarchate of Constantinople</b> in 1850, was a pillar of national identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander the Good (r. 1400–1432) organised the chancery, the fiscal system and the Church; in 1401 the <b>Patriarch of Constantinople</b> recognised a Moldavian metropolitan see at Suceava.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Polyeuctus was elected <b>Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople</b>, archbishop of the city and senior bishop of the Eastern Orthodox Church, and held the office until 970.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Nephon, Bishop of Cyzicus, was consecrated <b>Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-9">9 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1368 he reconciled his church with the <b>Patriarch of Constantinople</b>, ending the schism caused by Dušan's patriarchate.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-serres">Despotate of Serres</a></li><li><blockquote>Sratsimir ruled from Vidin's Baba Vida fortress over lands along the Danube, minting his own coins and placing his church under the <b>Patriarch of Constantinople</b> rather than Tarnovo.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsardom-of-vidin">Tsardom of Vidin</a></li></ul></section>
